triage: fix_game: The R1 test is correct. In Acorns Solo, Ober of Bells (trump strength 17) beats Unter of Hearts (14), so seat 2 should take the 5 points (49+5=54) and seat 1 stays at 50, below the 53 bid. The game gave the trick to seat 1, and the R12 failures show the same fault: trick resolution ignores the trump strengths.
step C: building the game file
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: The full-play tests (R2, R3) start from a real deal and ran without error, so trick play itself works. Only the four scenarios that inject a mid-hand state crashed. Those scenarios use internal names the test writer guessed (round_in_hand, leader, follower, hand, stack1_up, hand_points, a 'led card'), so the game's own bookkeeping, such as its led_card, did not match a card in its card table.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: revise_rules: The game was built faithfully, but the design exceeds the platform's budgets: 5 choice steps per round against a limit of 4, and 87 attributes against a limit of 80. It also has a degenerate strategy: always picking the first option wins about 70% of games for either seat, and always picking the last option loses over 80%.
